I am testing `recover_json_object` (open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object). Its documented rule says: “The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.”

The two replies are as follows. Reply A is exactly this text: a line containing three backticks followed by the word `json`, then a line containing `{"a": 1}`, then a line containing `{"b": 2}`, then a line containing three backticks, then a line containing `{"c": 3}`. Reply B is exactly this text: a line containing `{"c": 3}`.

For Reply A, the rule requires the answer `{"b": 2}`, because there is a code fence and the last object inside that fence is `{"b": 2}`; the later `{"c": 3}` outside the fence must be ignored. For Reply B, the rule requires `{"c": 3}`, because there is no fence and it is the last top-level object. The check `recover_json_object` returns `{"c": 3}` for both replies: in Reply A the fenced pool candidate is the whole fenced content `{"a": 1}\n{"b": 2}`, which does not parse as a single object, so the fenced pool is skipped and the top-level pool is used, picking the last top-level object `{"c": 3}`. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if `recover_json_object` returned `{"b": 2}` for Reply A, or if the documented rule were read as allowing the last top-level object outside a fence to win even when a fence holds a scoreable object.